1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,437
Meet OpsPulse, a mission control for service incidents.

2
00:00:05,417 --> 00:00:11,471
Built by Chhavi Ahlawat, this project combines a React interface, a FastAPI

3
00:00:11,501 --> 00:00:14,513
backend, and a Kubernetes delivery platform.

4
00:00:14,543 --> 00:00:19,431
Let's take a quick tour, then create and resolve a real demo

5
00:00:19,461 --> 00:00:19,840
incident.

6
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:27,610
The overview brings the important numbers together: active incidents, mean time to

7
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:29,637
resolve, and operational services.

8
00:00:29,617 --> 00:00:36,140
Scrolling down reveals incident trends, the severity breakdown, and recent activity.

9
00:00:36,170 --> 00:00:41,638
The example services get their health from incident records; this app does

10
00:00:41,668 --> 00:00:43,470
not probe external systems.

11
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:50,347
The incidents page keeps the response history in one searchable list.

12
00:00:50,327 --> 00:00:55,433
We can focus on active incidents, then narrow the list by severity

13
00:00:55,463 --> 00:00:56,717
or affected service.

14
00:00:56,747 --> 00:01:01,687
Each row shows the incident commander, current status, and how long the

15
00:01:01,717 --> 00:01:03,343
incident has been open.

16
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:10,271
Let's create a clearly labelled demo incident: checkout response delay.

17
00:01:10,251 --> 00:01:16,091
I'll select Checkout API, set the commander to Chhavi, and choose severity

18
00:01:16,121 --> 00:01:16,580
three.

19
00:01:16,610 --> 00:01:21,636
Submitting the form saves the incident through the API and opens its

20
00:01:21,666 --> 00:01:22,478
response timeline.

21
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:27,141
The incident starts in Investigating.

22
00:01:27,121 --> 00:01:31,484
First, I record that the cause has been identified.

23
00:01:31,514 --> 00:01:35,717
Next, I move it to Monitoring and note that the demo fix

24
00:01:35,747 --> 00:01:36,776
is being checked.

25
00:01:36,806 --> 00:01:39,518
Finally, I resolve it.

26
00:01:39,548 --> 00:01:45,034
The timeline preserves each message, author, and timestamp, so the full response

27
00:01:45,064 --> 00:01:46,873
is easy to follow.

28
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:53,599
The services page shows what the team operates, who owns it, and

29
00:01:53,629 --> 00:01:54,457
its criticality.

30
00:01:54,437 --> 00:01:59,456
Each card includes the current status and links to active incidents.

31
00:01:59,486 --> 00:02:02,878
Services can also be added or edited.

32
00:02:02,908 --> 00:02:07,805
I'll open the form briefly, without changing the existing catalogue.

33
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:14,196
The orbital theme is designed for a focused dashboard, with mint accents

34
00:02:14,226 --> 00:02:15,831
and an Earth backdrop.

35
00:02:15,811 --> 00:02:18,208
There is also a light mode.

36
00:02:18,238 --> 00:02:22,857
Switching themes keeps the same application data and workflow.

37
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:29,673
The public status page presents a simpler view for people following an

38
00:02:29,703 --> 00:02:30,095
issue.

39
00:02:30,075 --> 00:02:36,150
It shows current service impact and recent resolutions, including our demo incident.

40
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:41,866
Further down, the uptime history summarises downtime recorded by incidents.

41
00:02:41,896 --> 00:02:48,346
These are example services, not claims about real payment or identity providers.

42
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:56,036
Behind the interface, GitHub Actions tests the backend, builds the containers, and

43
00:02:56,066 --> 00:02:57,572
runs security checks.

44
00:02:57,552 --> 00:03:03,426
This completed pipeline also tested the Helm deployment in a temporary Kubernetes

45
00:03:03,456 --> 00:03:03,918
cluster.

46
00:03:03,948 --> 00:03:09,506
Its verified run passed all ten jobs, with thirty backend tests and

47
00:03:09,536 --> 00:03:11,369
ninety-seven percent backend coverage.

48
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:18,206
Argo CD connects the Git repository to the running Kubernetes application.

49
00:03:18,186 --> 00:03:22,512
The status here shows the application is synced and healthy.

50
00:03:22,542 --> 00:03:28,835
The resource tree links the frontend, backend, database, and supporting resources.

51
00:03:28,865 --> 00:03:34,733
When approved image tags change in Git, Argo CD reconciles the deployment.

52
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:41,056
Grafana turns Prometheus metrics into a readable dashboard.

53
00:03:41,036 --> 00:03:47,948
Here we can inspect request traffic, latency, active incidents, and backend availability.

54
00:03:47,978 --> 00:03:54,804
Scrolling down shows response codes, incident activity, and CPU and memory usage.

55
00:03:54,834 --> 00:04:00,751
These measurements come from this small demo workload, not a production benchmark.

56
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:06,364
Prometheus collects the measurements behind those graphs.

57
00:04:06,344 --> 00:04:11,338
Its target view lets us check whether the backend endpoints are being

58
00:04:11,368 --> 00:04:12,176
scraped successfully.

59
00:04:12,206 --> 00:04:16,945
This connects what we see in the dashboard to the running application.

60
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:25,590
That's OpsPulse: an incident workflow, automated delivery, and monitoring in one project.

61
00:04:25,570 --> 00:04:30,879
This walkthrough ran in the existing Codespaces Kubernetes environment.

62
00:04:30,909 --> 00:04:36,316
AWS infrastructure is prepared for a separate short demo, but no AWS

63
00:04:36,346 --> 00:04:39,035
resources were launched for this video.

64
00:04:39,065 --> 00:04:41,158
Thank you for watching.

